  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather will prevail this week \ncontinuing into the weekend with no significant weather hazards \nanticipated. \n\nHigh pressure will be in control through most of the week \nresulting in mostly clear/sunny skies. Following this morning's\nchilly weather temperatures will moderate later today through \nThursday as the flow turns southwesterly ahead of the next cold \nfront. In fact highs by Thursday afternoon look to reach the \nmiddle to upper 70s for most of the area, perhaps even touching\n80, though latest NBM has nudged the temps down a bit. The cold\nfront moves through by late Thursday as a dry front and will be\nfollowed by high pressure re- establishing itself over the area\nfor Friday through at least Saturday with seasonable \ntemperatures. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The remnants of what is now Tropical Storm \nIsaias will approach the area later this weekend bringing \nchances for rain as early as Sunday.\n\nHigh pressure shifts offshore by Sunday as the next low \npressure system (what is now Tropical Storm Isaias) approaches \nfrom the south and west. There are some model differences \nregarding this system and its arrival time in the area. The \nlatest ECMWF slowed down a bit compared to its earlier run, \nwhile the GFS has sped up. In any event, the system should be \nfairly progressive but will likely have significant moisture \nwith it (PWATs potentially reaching a rather tropical 2 inches) \nso the rain could be heavy at times with the potential for some \nareas to see 1-2 inches.",
